Design decisions (bake these in)

1) PromptContextBuilder (pure class, domain.ai).

   class PromptContextBuilder(
       private val systemPrompt: String = DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT,
       private val maxOutputTokens: Int = DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS,
   ) {
       /** Assembles the MINIMAL outbound request: one USER message + a static system prompt. No
        *  device/usage/calendar/location/history context is added (Fork P5-3, fail-closed). The
        *  userCommand is passed through verbatim and is the ONLY user-controlled content that leaves. */
       fun build(userCommand: String): AiRequest =
           AiRequest(
               messages = listOf(AiMessage(AiRole.USER, userCommand)),
               system = systemPrompt,
               maxOutputTokens = maxOutputTokens,
           )
       companion object {
           const val DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT = "<short, static, context-free assistant instruction>"
           const val DEFAULT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS = 512   // phone-reasonable guidance, not a hard pin
       }
   }

DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT: a short, static, context-free instruction (e.g. identify as the Sidr
launcher assistant, answer concisely). It must contain no denylist term and no device/user
context. No model name baked in (free-text model is config — Block K). model is left null (the
engine/router uses its configured default); do not pin a model here.
Keep the public surface tiny: build(userCommand) only. No overload that accepts a context bag —
adding context is a deliberate future change, not an open door.


2) OutboundContextPolicy (object, domain.ai) — the explicit allow-list + denylists.

   object OutboundContextPolicy {
       /** Positive allow-list: the ONLY context categories permitted to leave the device. Adding an
        *  entry is a privacy decision (Fork P5-3). Forbidden categories are deliberately ABSENT. */
       enum class AllowedContext { USER_COMMAND, STATIC_SYSTEM_PROMPT, GENERATION_LIMITS }
       val ALLOWED: Set<AllowedContext> = setOf(
           AllowedContext.USER_COMMAND,
           AllowedContext.STATIC_SYSTEM_PROMPT,
           AllowedContext.GENERATION_LIMITS,
       )
       /** Forbidden CONTEXT-source terms (Phase-4 family + context-leak family). Scanned over the
        *  static system prompt + the field/category inventories — NEVER over the user command. */
       val FORBIDDEN_CONTEXT_TERMS: List<String> = listOf(
           "voice","location","calendar","history","conversation","transcript",
           "usage","contacts","sms","email","clipboard",
       )
       /** Credential terms. Scanned over OUTBOUND_FIELD_NAMES to prove no field can carry a key.
        *
        *  DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED — "token":
        *  the Phase-4 credential family includes "token", but the legitimate outbound field
        *  `maxOutputTokens` CONTAINS the substring "token" (lower-cased "maxoutputtokens"), so a
        *  substring scan with "token" present would vacuously fail on a non-credential field. We drop
        *  "token" and keep the meaningful credential family below — exactly the Phase-4 "key"-excluded
        *  precedent (a term dropped because it collides with a legitimate identifier). The retained
        *  terms still fire if a real credential-named field is ever added (e.g. apiKey / bearerToken /
        *  authorization). */
       val CREDENTIAL_TERMS: List<String> = listOf(
           "secret","apikey","api_key","bearer","authorization","credential","password",
       )
       /** Hand-synced inventory of AiRequest's outbound field names (Phase-4 TABLE_NAMES precedent).
        *  RULE: adding a field to AiRequest requires updating this set — the guard enforces it stays
        *  credential-free. */
       val OUTBOUND_FIELD_NAMES: Set<String> = setOf("messages","system","maxOutputTokens","model","stopSequences")
   }

Keep query/search/message/api Phase-4 terms OUT of the lists where they would false-match a
legitimate token: "message" collides with AiMessage/messages, "token" collides with maxOutputTokens
(see the excluded-note above), "system" is itself a legitimate field/category name. Document every
term deliberately excluded and why (mirror the Phase-4 "key"-excluded note) so the guards stay
meaningful and don't vacuously fail. Before committing, sanity-check that NO retained CREDENTIAL_TERM
and NO retained FORBIDDEN_CONTEXT_TERM is a substring of any entry in OUTBOUND_FIELD_NAMES, of any
AllowedContext name, or of the DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT — if one is, either rename the colliding term out
or document it as excluded. (Confirmed non-collisions to assert, not assume: "authorization" is NOT a
substring of the AiError variant `Unauthorized` — endings differ, …orized vs …orization — so the
credential scan over AiError.toString() does not vacuously fire on Unauthorized.)


3) Guards are reflection-free. Use the hand-synced OUTBOUND_FIELD_NAMES set + literal denylist
scans + constructed sentinel values. Do not add kotlin-reflect.

Steps

 L1 PromptContextBuilder + OutboundContextPolicy in domain.ai (per decisions 1–2). Pure.
 L2 AiRequestGuardTest (domain/src/test, JVM):
- ALLOWED contains no forbidden context category (structural: none of the allow-listed
categories is a forbidden source).
- DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT (and any builder-injected static text) contains no
FORBIDDEN_CONTEXT_TERMS (denylist scan over static text only).
- Pass-through / no-injection: build("<<SENTINEL_CMD>>") yields exactly one USER message
whose content == "<<SENTINEL_CMD>>" (verbatim, unmodified), system == DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT,
model == null, no extra messages, no ASSISTANT/system-as-message smuggling. Proves the
builder adds no hidden context.
- User content is allowed through: build("open my calendar") keeps "calendar" in the USER
message (assert the denylist is NOT applied to user content — a regression guard against an
over-eager filter that would corrupt legitimate commands).
 L3 OutboundSecretLeakGuardTest (domain/src/test, JVM):
- OUTBOUND_FIELD_NAMES contains no CREDENTIAL_TERMS substring (no AiRequest field can carry a
key) — and assert the inventory matches AiRequest's real fields (hand-sync check: list the
expected names; if AiRequest gains a field this test must be updated — document the rule). This
passes precisely because "token" was excluded from CREDENTIAL_TERMS (see the policy note); add a
short comment in the test pointing at that exclusion so a future reader doesn't "fix" it by
re-adding "token" and breaking the build on maxOutputTokens.
- Each AiError variant constructed with a safe detail has a toString() that does not
contain a planted secret sentinel (the sentinel was never inserted — proves the surface has no
place to hold it) and contains no CREDENTIAL_TERMS field name.
- An AiRequest built from a sentinel command + a sentinel "FAKE-KEY" never placed in it →
toString() excludes "FAKE-KEY" (the key has no field to live in).
 L4 Verification + guards (below); ADR; advance CLAUDE.md to Block L.

